{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"IMPLEMENTED (pending full-suite + push). Recording the review finding that shaped it and three things the local gates caught, because two of them are traps for anyone writing subprocess tests in this repo.\n\n## The re-review's one blocking finding was RIGHT, and cheap to have missed\nThe reviewer blocked on the plan omitting how the Markdown reaches pandoc's stdin: the landed `_convert` passes `input=markdown` to `subprocess.run`, while the ACLI pattern the plan cites spawns with `stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL` (acli takes no stdin). An implementer mirroring ACLI verbatim would have fed pandoc an EMPTY document — every unit failing its preservation check and degrading to raw Markdown, while every process-lifetime test still passed. The plan now states `stdin=subprocess.PIPE` + `communicate(input=…, timeout=…)`, and the code has two tests on it: one pinning `stdin == PIPE` and `!= DEVNULL` plus the exact argv, and one \"guards the guard\" test proving `_convert(\"\")` differs from `_convert(unit)` — otherwise the first test would pass under the broken implementation too. Re-review after the edit: **PASS, 0 blocking, 6 advisory.**\n\n## TRAP 1: do not monkeypatch `subprocess.Popen` in this repo's tests\nMy first version patched the module attribute to inject a fake pandoc. The test passed and then HUNG in teardown, surfacing as a 100s pytest-timeout inside `selectors.poll(None)` — which reads like infrastructure, not like a test bug. Cause: the autouse `_no_repo_commits` fixture in `tests/conftest.py` shells out to git during teardown via `tests/_isolation.py`, and `monkeypatch` had not yet undone the patch, so **the conftest's git call spawned my fake instead**, then blocked on its pipes forever.\n\nThe fix is better than the workaround: hand `_convert` a path to a REAL executable stand-in (a chmod +x script) instead of patching the spawn. Everything stays real — argv, pipes, timeout, reap — and nothing else in the process is affected. Anyone testing a subprocess seam here should do the same.\n\n## TRAP 2: the stand-in's grandchild holds the pipes, so never hand its pid back on stderr\nThe whole point of the stand-in is a grandchild that INHERITS the pipes and spins. A test that reads the pid off `proc.stderr` waits for EOF, and EOF needs every holder gone — so if the reap ever regresses, the test DEADLOCKS instead of failing. The pid now comes back through a file.\n\n## TRAP 3: `check_wall_clock_asserts` wants its marker on the line DIRECTLY above\n`assert elapsed < 60` is a legitimate hang-guard (it separates \"the 1s timeout fired\" from \"we waited out the stand-in's 300s sleep\" — the only two ways `_convert` can return there). The gate accepts `# timing: hang-guard — <reason>` only within one line of the assert; a marker at the top of a multi-line comment block is NOT seen.\n\n## Corpus byte-identity: FULL, not sampled\nI initially strided the equivalence comparison, believing the full corpus would cost ~9 minutes. That estimate was wrong — it was measuring the Popen-patching hang, not the work. Measured properly: **~880 renderable units rendered twice (new path + a transcript of the pre-hardening one), all byte-identical, in 89s** — the same order as the existing corpus suite. So the AC is met literally, over the whole committed 178-body corpus, with no sampling.\n\n## Delivered\n`wiki_render.py` (own `Popen` + `start_new_session=True` + caller-side `communicate(input=…, timeout=…)` + `_reap_pandoc` over `rebar._proc.reap_process_group`, per-unit fallback unchanged); `reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s` across the four-file config surface (schema + validator, auto-derived `REBAR_RECONCILER_DC_PANDOC_TIMEOUT_S`, regenerated `tests/golden/config_surface.json`, `docs/config.md`); a NEW repo-root `NOTICE` recording pandoc 3.6.1 / GPL-2.0-or-later / upstream and how it reaches an install; a CI `pandoc pin + NOTICE provenance` gate that fails on a loosened pin, a multi-version pin, a missing NOTICE, or a NOTICE that no longer names the pinned wheel or its licence. 14 new tests; `make lint`, `make typecheck`, complexity baseline (active=53 new=0 increased=0), and the `check_*` gates all clean.\n\nNote the wheel was ALREADY pinned `pypandoc-binary==1.15` by `repressive-delectable-swan` in two places, so this story adds the VERIFICATION rather than the pin."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786615666081844001,"uuid":"940dade2-a0b0-42c0-851d-1aefbc32318f"}